# Math notation

All mathematical expressions MUST be written in LaTeX, never as Unicode
symbols, plain-text fractions, or mixed ASCII. This applies to:

- Assistant chat messages (use `$...$` inline, `$$...$$` for display).
- Markdown files I edit/create (same delimiters).
- Docstrings / comments when they contain equations.

## Examples

Bad (do not produce):

- `P(rank <= i)`
- `O^(l,h)_i = Σ_j A_ij * V_j`
- `R_KL ≈ 0.27`
- `KL(P || Q)`
- top-1 / top-10 (when used as formal notation)

Good (always produce):

- $P(\mathrm{rank} \leq i)$
- $O^{(\ell, h)}_i = \sum_j A^{(\ell,h)}_{ij} V^{(\ell,h)}_j$
- $R_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx 0.27$
- $\mathrm{KL}(P \,\|\, Q)$
- $\text{top-}1$ / $\text{top-}10$

## Rules

- Operators like $\leq, \geq, \approx, \neq, \in, \to$ — always LaTeX,
  never `<=`, `>=`, `≈`, `≠`, `∈`, `→` in prose.
- Subscripts/superscripts with multiple characters always in braces:
  $R_{\mathrm{KL}}$, not $R_KL$.
- Use `\mathrm` for multi-letter operators/labels (KL, JS, rank, argmax,
  softmax) inside math mode.
- Use `\,\|\,` for the double-bar in KL divergences.
- Use `\ell` for layer index, not plain $l$.
- Tables and inline ratios: still LaTeX-ify numeric references when they
  name a metric, e.g. $R_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0.27$ rather than "R_KL = 0.27".
